  "notes": "University Park. Major code 'MATSCI' avoids colliding with the MATSE department prefix. Historical-year build (2026-09-17), fetched the real archived bulletin at the URL in 'source' above (raw HTML via curl, cross-checked against the page's own printed semester/fall/spring subtotals and 'minimum of 131 credits' program-requirements total, not WebFetch's summary) and compared it course-by-course against MATSCI-2026.json's already-verified structure. Program Requirements course list is byte-for-byte identical to 2025 and 2026 (confirmed via structured diff of all 67 course-list rows): same MATSE 436, same Processing Laboratory options (MATSE 463/468/471/473 -- the current numbering, NOT the older 472/474 seen on the 2022/2023 archived pages), same 5-course Synthesis and Processing / Structure and Characterization / Properties elective lists (no leftover unresolved 448/444 entries -- those are gone by this edition), same Entrance-to-Major requirements, same 12-credit Technical Elective pool, and the same 131-credit minimum. Re-verified the CHEM 110 -> MATH 22/21/4/3 prereq chain against the adjacent 2022 archived edition's own MATH/CHEM course-description pages (wording identical to the current catalog, and this app's remedial-chain fix logic doesn't change year to year for a stable Penn State math-placement sequence) -- so this file carries over the 2026 plan's MATH 3/4/21/22 additions and 21-credit/semester cap unchanged. This year's Suggested Academic Plan does show the same MATH 220-credits-printed-as-3 inconsistency seen on the 2023 page (Second Year Fall subtotal prints 18); kept MATH 220 at its real 2 credits here too, for the same reason given in the 2023 file's notes. Sequencing note (observed, not applied): this year's own Suggested Academic Plan still places both 'IE 424 or STAT 401' and 'MATSE 413' together in Second Year Spring (this file's Semester 4) -- the same as 2022/2023, and still one edition before the 2025 bulletin moves MATSE 413 to Third Year Spring. Kept this file's placement identical to 2026's (Semester 6 for MATSE 413, Semester 7 for IE 424/STAT 401) for the same reasons given in the 2022/2023 files' notes: neither course carries a tracked prerequisite, 0 scheduling warnings either way, and the 2026 file's placement is already a deliberate credit-cap rearrangement rather than a literal per-year mirror.",
